XOR gives a `1` wherever the two input bits are different. Therefore, after calculating `start ^ goal`, the answer is just the number of set bits in that result.

The loop reads the last bit with `& 1` and then shifts the value. When `start` and `goal` are equal, the XOR result is zero and the method returns zero immediately.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minBitFlips(int start, int goal) {
        int differentBits = start ^ goal;
        int flips = 0;

        while (differentBits != 0) {
            flips += differentBits & 1;
            differentBits >>>= 1;
        }

        return flips;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of bits in the XOR result.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The two numbers can be checked one bit at a time. The lowest bits are compared first, then both values are shifted right. If the bits are different, one flip is needed.

For example, with `start = 10` and `goal = 7`, the binary values are `1010` and `0111`. The comparison finds three different positions. The loop also continues when only one number still has bits left.

```cpp
class Solution{   
public:    
    int minBitFlips(int start, int goal) { 
        int flips = 0;

        while (start > 0 || goal > 0) {
            if ((start & 1) != (goal & 1)) {
                flips++;
            }

            start >>= 1;
            goal >>= 1;
        }

        return flips;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of bits checked.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

After XORing the numbers, every set bit marks one required flip. Instead of checking every position, `value &= value - 1` removes one set bit at a time.

For a dry run, `start = 10` and `goal = 7` produce `10 ^ 7 = 13`, which is `1101` in binary. The clearing operation runs three times, matching the three required flips.

```cpp
class Solution{   
public:    
    int minBitFlips(int start, int goal) { 
        int differentBits = start ^ goal;
        int flips = 0;

        while (differentBits != 0) {
            differentBits &= differentBits - 1;
            flips++;
        }

        return flips;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(p)`, where `p` is the number of set bits in `start ^ goal`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

## Intuition

XOR is the "difference detector." 
Modern CPUs have a dedicated hardware instruction to count set bits instantly. GCC (the standard C++ compiler) exposes this via a built-in function called __builtin_popcount().


## Approach
We take the xor to see the how may bits are different and count them with __builtin_popcount().

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{   
public:    
    int minBitsFlip(int start, int goal) { 
        return __builtin_popcount(start^goal);
    }
};
```

